<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="45–55 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Vocabulary · Lesson 12</p>
  <h1>Daily routines &amp; <em>telling the time</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Talk about a typical day from morning to night. Learn routine chunks such as <span class="ex">wake up</span>, <span class="ex">have breakfast</span>, and <span class="ex">go to work</span>, then connect each one to a clear time: <span class="ex">I get up at six thirty.</span> <span class="ex">She starts work at nine.</span></p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">45–55 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">routine chunks and sequence</span>
    <span class="tag">digital and past/to clock styles</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min optional listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Compare two fictional schedules.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Read the clock; choose at or in.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Match times, choose, and build sentences.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Present a fictional weekday and weekend.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">Routine verbs follow the present-simple system. Compare <span class="ex">I get / she gets</span>, <span class="ex">I go / she goes</span>, <span class="ex">I have / she has</span>, and <span class="ex">I finish / she finishes</span>. The <a href="/lessons/a1/present-simple/">present simple lesson</a> teaches the complete spelling and question system; here you use it inside routine chunks.</p>

