    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/car.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">drive</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/train.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">take the train</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/shopping-cart.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">go shopping</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/television.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">watch TV</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/phone.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">check my phone</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/notebook.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">study</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice before the rule</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Two mornings, side by side</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">"I get up at 6:30. I take a shower and have breakfast. I go to work at 8:00."</span><br /><span class="ex">"She gets up at 7:00. She has breakfast at home. She starts work at 9:00."</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>The verbs change a little between the two people. Compare <span class="ex">I get up</span> and <span class="ex">She gets up</span>. What is added for <strong>she</strong>?</li>
      <li>Each action has a clock time. Which small word comes right before <span class="ex">6:30</span>, <span class="ex">8:00</span>, and <span class="ex">9:00</span>?</li>
      <li>How would you read <span class="ex">6:30</span> out loud? And <span class="ex">9:00</span>? Try both before you read on.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You matched each digital clock to the requested o’clock, half past, quarter past, or quarter to phrase.">
    <h3>Discovery check: read the clock, then choose</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Choose the requested past/to-style phrase. You will compare it with common U.S. number-style times next.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="o'clock">o'clock</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="half past">half past</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="quarter past">quarter past</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="quarter to">quarter to</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>seven <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="o'clock" data-hint="A time with :00 can use o’clock after the hour." data-fix="7:00 is seven o’clock." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(7:00)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span><button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="half past" data-hint="Thirty minutes after seven is half past seven." data-fix="7:30 is half past seven." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> seven &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(7:30)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span><button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="quarter past" data-hint="Fifteen minutes after seven is quarter past seven." data-fix="7:15 is quarter past seven." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> seven &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(7:15)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span><button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="quarter to" data-hint="7:45 is fifteen minutes before eight, so it is quarter to eight." data-fix="7:45 is quarter to eight." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> eight &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(7:45)</span></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>The system: two ways to read the clock</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>U.S. English commonly uses <strong>number style</strong>: say the hour first and then the minutes. <span class="ex">6:30</span> is <span class="ex">six thirty</span>, and <span class="ex">8:45</span> is <span class="ex">eight forty-five</span>. The traditional <strong>past/to style</strong> is also correct and widely understood. In that style, describe the minutes in relation to the hour.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>clock</th><th>common number style</th><th>past/to style</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>8:00</b></td><td><b>eight / eight o’clock</b></td><td><b>eight o’clock</b></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>8:15</b></td><td><b>eight fifteen</b></td><td><b>quarter past eight</b></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>8:30</b></td><td><b>eight thirty</b></td><td><b>half past eight</b></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>8:45</b></td><td><b>eight forty-five</b></td><td><b>quarter to nine</b></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>For minutes from <span class="ex">01</span> through <span class="ex">09</span>, number style normally says <span class="ex">oh</span>: <span class="ex">8:05 = eight oh five</span>. Add <span class="ex">a.m.</span> for a time before noon and <span class="ex">p.m.</span> for a time after noon when the part of day is not already clear: <span class="ex">7:00 a.m.</span> and <span class="ex">7:00 p.m.</span>.</p>
  <p class="note-line">Use <span class="ex">o’clock</span> only with an exact hour ending in <span class="ex">:00</span>. Say <span class="ex">at nine o’clock</span>, <span class="ex">at nine a.m.</span>, or <span class="ex">at nine in the morning</span>. Avoid the redundant combination <span class="ex">nine o’clock a.m.</span>.</p>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Meaning check</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Does <span class="ex">quarter to nine</span> mean 9:15? <strong>No.</strong> <em>To</em> means before, so it is <span class="ex">8:45</span>. <span class="ex">Quarter past</span> is after the hour.</li>
      <li>Is <span class="ex">half past six</span> the same as <span class="ex">6:30</span>? <strong>Yes.</strong> <em>Half past</em> always means thirty minutes after the hour.</li>
      <li>What about <span class="ex">12:00</span>? Use <span class="ex">noon</span> for the middle of the day and <span class="ex">midnight</span> for the middle of the night. These words avoid possible confusion between <span class="ex">12 a.m.</span> and <span class="ex">12 p.m.</span>.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>The system: routine chunks across the day</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Learn these as whole chunks and keep their small words together. The order below is one fictional weekday, not a rule about how every person lives.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>part of day</th><th>routine chunk</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>early morning</b></td><td><b>wake up · get up · take a shower</b></td><td><span class="contraction">I wake up at 6:30.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>morning</b></td><td><b>have breakfast · go to work · start work</b></td><td><span class="contraction">I start work at 9:00.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>midday</b></td><td><b>have lunch · take a break</b></td><td><span class="contraction">I have lunch at noon.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>afternoon / evening</b></td><td><b>finish work · get home · have dinner</b></td><td><span class="contraction">I finish work at 5:00.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>night</b></td><td><b>relax · go to bed</b></td><td><span class="contraction">I go to bed at 11:00.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>A routine is a repeated pattern</h3>
    <p>The present simple describes what usually happens, not necessarily what is happening at this moment. A routine can happen <span class="ex">every weekday</span>, <span class="ex">on Saturdays</span>, or only <span class="ex">sometimes</span>: <span class="ex">Maya usually starts work at 8:45. On Saturdays, she does not work.</span></p>
    <p>Sequence the pattern so another person can follow it: <span class="ex">First, Maya wakes up. Then she gets up. After that, she has breakfast. Finally, she leaves home.</span></p>
  </div>

  <h3>Similar chunks do different jobs</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>contrast</th><th>meaning</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>wake up / get up</b></td><td>stop sleeping / leave the bed</td><td>I wake up at 6:30, but I get up at 6:45.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>go to work / start work</b></td><td>travel there / begin working</td><td>She goes to work at eight and starts work at nine.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>finish work / get home</b></td><td>stop working / arrive at home</td><td>He finishes work at five and gets home at six.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">Say <span class="ex">go to work</span> and <span class="ex">go to school</span>, but say <span class="ex">go home</span> and <span class="ex">get home</span> with no <span class="ex">to</span>. For meals in general, use no article: <span class="ex">have breakfast, have lunch, have dinner</span>.</p>
  <p>Some routine chunks change a possessive word with the person: <span class="ex">I brush my teeth</span>, <span class="ex">Maya brushes her teeth</span>, and <span class="ex">Omar checks his phone</span>. If a fictional profile does not specify gender, singular <span class="ex">their</span> is natural: <span class="ex">Alex checks their phone</span>.</p>

  <h3>Place the time expression</h3>
  <p>Use <span class="ex">at</span> before a clock time, and <span class="ex">in</span> before a general part of the day:</p>
  <p><span class="ex">at 7:00 &nbsp; · &nbsp; at noon &nbsp; · &nbsp; at midnight</span><br /><span class="ex">in the morning &nbsp; · &nbsp; in the afternoon &nbsp; · &nbsp; in the evening</span><br /><span class="ex">at night</span> &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(this one uses at, not in)</span></p>
  <p>Use <span class="ex">on</span> before a named day: <span class="ex">on Monday</span>. Do not add a preposition before <span class="ex">every</span>, <span class="ex">this</span>, or <span class="ex">last</span>: <span class="ex">every morning, this afternoon, last night</span>. The exact start of morning, afternoon, and evening can vary, so add a clock time when precision matters.</p>

  <h3>Ask and answer about a routine time</h3>
  <p>Use <span class="ex">What time do + I/you/we/they + base verb?</span> and <span class="ex">What time does + he/she/it + base verb?</span> After <span class="ex">does</span>, the routine verb returns to its base form.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>question</th><th>short answer</th><th>complete answer</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>What time do you start work?</td><td>At nine.</td><td>I start work at nine.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What time does Maya start work?</td><td>At nine.</td><td>She starts work at nine.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">Compare the verb forms: <span class="ex">Maya starts</span>, but <span class="ex">What time does Maya start?</span>, not <span class="ex">does Maya starts</span>.</p>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: join the routine and make the time clear</h3>
    <p>Routine phrases work as spoken chunks. The small words become light, while the action and clock time carry the useful beats.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>chunk</th><th>natural rhythm</th><th>what to notice</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>get up</td><td><span class="contraction">I get <strong>UP</strong> at six thirty.</span></td><td>say <span class="ex">get-up</span> smoothly; the linked <span class="ex">t</span> can sound like a quick <span class="ex">d</span> in U.S. speech</td></tr>
        <tr><td>go to work</td><td><span class="contraction">I go to <strong>WORK</strong> at eight.</span></td><td><span class="ex">to</span> is usually unstressed /tə/</td></tr>
        <tr><td>have breakfast</td><td><span class="contraction">I have <strong>BREAK</strong>fast at seven.</span></td><td><span class="ex">breakfast</span> has two spoken parts; the first is strong</td></tr>
        <tr><td>quarter to nine</td><td><span class="contraction">a <strong>QUAR</strong>ter to <strong>NINE</strong></span></td><td>in American speech, the middle <span class="ex">t</span> in <span class="ex">quarter</span> can sound close to a soft <span class="ex">d</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Say number-style times in two groups: <span class="ex">6:30 → six thirty</span>, <span class="ex">8:45 → eight forty-five</span>. For <span class="ex">a.m.</span> and <span class="ex">p.m.</span>, say each letter name: <span class="ex">seven A-M · seven P-M</span>.</p>
    <p>Keep the third-person ending audible: <span class="ex">gets</span> ends /s/, <span class="ex">goes</span> and <span class="ex">has</span> end /z/, and <span class="ex">finishes</span> adds a final syllable /ɪz/. In <span class="ex">What time does Maya start?</span>, <span class="ex">does</span> can be light, but <span class="ex">start</span> stays in the base form.</p>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read routine time, person, and connected-speech check">
    <h2>Listen: which time or person did you hear?</h2>
    <div class="lead-rule"></div>
    <p>Your teacher reads each fictional line once with natural U.S. rhythm and once more slowly. Listen for exact minutes, a.m. or p.m., and whether the question asks about the listener or another person. Open the script only after checking.</p>
    <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You distinguished close clock times, morning and evening labels, and do versus does routine questions in connected speech.">
      <h3>Tutor-read listening check</h3>
      <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Routine listening meanings">
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="6:15 a.m.">6:15 a.m.</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="6:50 a.m.">6:50 a.m.</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="8:45 a.m.">8:45 a.m.</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="8:14 a.m.">8:14 a.m.</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="5:30 p.m.">5:30 p.m.</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="asks about the listener">asks about the listener</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="asks about Maya">asks about Maya</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a routine at night">a routine at night</button>
      </div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Clip 1 gives <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="6:15 a.m." data-hint="Listen for six fifteen, not six fifty, followed by A-M." data-fix="I wake up at six fifteen A-M: 6:15 a.m." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Clip 2 gives <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="8:45 a.m." data-hint="Listen for eight forty-five, not eight fourteen, followed by A-M." data-fix="She starts work at eight forty-five A-M: 8:45 a.m." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Clip 3 gives <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="5:30 p.m." data-hint="Five thirty followed by P-M places the routine after noon." data-fix="He finishes work at five thirty P-M: 5:30 p.m." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
